# Cascade — Debug and automation API

Cascade ships a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code, without touching a pointer or waiting on real time. It is
what you use to iterate on the rules, reproduce a specific board, drive the
victory cascade to a known point, script a scenario, and
capture clean screenshots of an exact game state. This file defines that surface.
Implement all of it, on the same footing as the game itself.

Nothing here changes how a person plays. The debug API is inert during normal
play, doing nothing until something calls it, and the debug overlay is off until
toggled.

## A deterministic core

The whole surface rests on the game being deterministic and, where it moves over
time, steppable. Two properties make it driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state (the piles, a move, the win check, and the victory
  cascade) advances without depending on a canvas, on `requestAnimationFrame`, or
  on wall-clock time to make progress. Rendering reads the state, never the other
  way around.
- Seeded randomness. The randomness the game uses, the deal shuffle and the
  cascade's launch velocities, runs off a seedable generator. Seeding it and
  replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. Normal play seeds
  from a non-deterministic source at each new game, so no two deals are alike; a
  caller that wants a repeatable board seeds it explicitly through `reset`.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of API calls and steps, the game reaches
the same state every time.

## The deterministic manual clock

Only one part of Cascade moves over time: the victory cascade, whose cards fall
and bounce on a fixed timestep of 120 Hz — one step is exactly `1/120` of a second
of game time (`specs/victory.md`). The dealing and every move happen instantly in
response to input, so they need no clock. The manual clock controls that one
time-driven system.

The game holds an `autoStep` flag, default `true` (normal human play). While
`autoStep` is true, the animation-frame loop advances the victory cascade from the
wall clock, so a person watching a won game sees the cards fall in real time.
While it is false, the loop still renders every frame but does not advance the
cascade on its own; `step(ticks)` becomes the only way it advances, so a scripted
cascade is exact and reproducible regardless of machine load.

`reset` and `step` set `autoStep = false`, beginning a driver-clocked session.
`setAutoStep(true)` hands the clock back to the game so the cascade runs itself in
real time again (useful for watching or recording a clip); `setAutoStep(false)`
returns to manual. This is an ordinary "advance it by hand" versus "let it run"
debugging affordance and is the only thing the flag governs; the control and input
operations below never change it.

## The `window.__cascade` object

Expose the API as a single object on the global `window.__cascade`, installed once
the game is running. It carries a `version` number (use `1`) and the operations
below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and arrays so a caller can read
them directly; positions are in the logical-pixel space of `specs/overview.md`.

Throughout, a card is written as a plain object `{ suit, rank, faceUp }`, where
`suit` is one of `"spades"`, `"hearts"`, `"diamonds"`, `"clubs"`, `rank` is `1`
(Ace) through `13` (King), and `faceUp` is a boolean. A pile is an array of cards
ordered bottom to top, so the last element is the card on top.

### Core operations

- `reset(options)` returns the game to its title state. `options` is optional, and
  `options.seed` (a number) seeds all of the game's randomness (the next deal's
  shuffle and the cascade's launches) so a scenario replays identically. Re-arms
  the manual clock (`autoStep = false`).
- `newGame()` deals a fresh game and enters play, exactly as choosing `NEW GAME`
  would. The deal uses the current seed, so `reset({ seed })` then `newGame()`
  produces a repeatable board.
- `step(ticks)` advances the victory cascade by exactly `ticks` fixed steps
  immediately, running the fixed-timestep update internally rather than waiting for
  real frames. The unit is whole simulation ticks, not seconds: the timestep is
  120 Hz, so one tick is `1/120` of a second, `step(1)` runs a single cascade step
  and `step(120)` advances one second of game time. Nothing is rounded or
  approximated — the number of steps asked for is the number of steps run.
  `ticks` must be a non-negative integer; `step(0)` is legal and does nothing,
  while a fractional or negative value is invalid and the call fails loudly rather
  than guessing what was meant. On any screen other than a running cascade it has
  no effect. Sets `autoStep = false`.
- `snapshot()` returns a plain, JSON-serializable object describing the current
  game state (see [Snapshot shape](#snapshot-shape)). It is a pure read and never
  changes anything.
- `setAutoStep(enabled)` sets the manual clock (see above): `true` lets the game
  advance the cascade in real time, `false` returns to manual stepping.

### Control operations

These set up a specific situation, then let the real systems resolve it. Each one
routes through the same code normal play uses; they arrange the board and invoke
real moves rather than fabricating an outcome.

- `setBoard(state)` places an exact board as a precondition and enters play.
  `state` may carry any of `stock`, `waste`, `foundations` (an array of four
  piles), and `tableau` (an array of seven piles), each given as arrays of cards.
  Any pile not named is left empty. A card's `faceUp` may be given per card; when
  omitted it defaults to face-up for the waste and foundations, face-down for the
  stock, and as-written for the tableau. This only arranges the cards; it decides
  nothing about whether a later move is legal.
- `turnStock()` clicks the stock: it turns the deal mode's turn count of cards
  face-up onto the waste, or, when the stock is empty, recycles the whole waste
  back into the stock (`specs/rules.md`). It runs the real
  stock code.
- `selectMenu(index)` selects and activates the title-menu item at `index`,
  exactly as clicking it would. The title menu lists its items in the order
  `specs/states.md` gives — `0` is `NEW GAME` and `1` is `HOW TO PLAY`, the same
  items the snapshot's `menuIndex` reports — so a caller opens a menu screen by
  index rather than by guessing where the build happens to draw the item (the menu
  layout is the build's own). It routes through the same activation a tap or click
  drives, so activating `NEW GAME` deals a fresh game and `HOW TO PLAY` opens the
  how-to screen. It has no effect on any screen other than the title, and an
  `index` that is not a whole number in range fails loudly rather than guessing.
- `move(source, target)` attempts a real move from one pile to another and returns
  `true` if the move was accepted, `false` if it was rejected. It asks the game's
  own legal-move check and, when the move is legal, applies it through the same
  path a drag-and-drop release uses (including turning a newly exposed column card
  face-up and detecting a win). A rejected move leaves the board unchanged.
  - `source` is `{ pile: "waste" }`, `{ pile: "foundation", index }`, or
    `{ pile: "tableau", column, row }`. For a tableau source, `row` is the index of
    the grabbed card (0 is the bottom-most, buried card; the top card has the
    highest index): the grabbed card and every card above it in the column move
    together as a run. When `row` is omitted, the column's top card is grabbed
    alone.
  - `target` is `{ pile: "foundation", index }` or `{ pile: "tableau", column }`.
- `autoMove(source)` performs the double-click auto-move (`specs/rules.md`): it
  sends a playable card straight to the foundation it belongs on when that move is
  legal, and does nothing otherwise. Returns `true` if the card went home. `source`
  is `{ pile: "waste" }` or `{ pile: "tableau", column }` (the column's top
  face-up card).

A typical check calls `reset({ seed })`, `setBoard(...)` to arrange the exact
piles wanted, then `move(...)` / `autoMove(...)` / `turnStock()` and reads the
result from `snapshot()`.

### Input operations

The control operations above drive the game through its rules layer. The API can
also inject pointer input, so a caller can drive the game the way a player does:
tap a menu item, tap the stock, and drag a card across the table. Injected
input flows through the same pointer handling a real mouse or touchscreen feeds,
exercising the actual hit-testing and drag-and-drop from `specs/states.md` rather
than a parallel path. Coordinates are in the logical 1280 x 720 space of
`specs/overview.md`.

- `pointerDown(x, y)` presses the pointer down at a logical point, beginning a
  press (which may become a drag).
- `pointerMove(x, y)` moves the pointer to a logical point; while a press is
  active this drags the held card or run, which follows the cursor and floats above
  the table, and highlights a legal drop target under it.
- `pointerUp(x, y)` releases the pointer at a logical point, completing a drag onto
  a legal target or returning the cards to their origin.
- `click(x, y)` is a convenience tap, a `pointerDown` immediately followed by a
  `pointerUp` at the same point. This activates a menu item, turns the stock, or
  triggers a HUD control.
- `doubleClick(x, y)` performs the double-click auto-move at a logical point,
  through the same handler a mouse's double-click or a double-tap uses.

The usual shape for an input-driven scenario is to `pointerDown` on a card,
`pointerMove` over a target so the held card follows and the target highlights,
then `pointerUp` to drop it, reading `snapshot()` to see where the cards ended up.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns an object with at least these fields:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "won",
  dealMode: <string>,        // this build's deal-mode id (see specs/rules.md)
  turnCount: <number>,       // cards the stock turns per click (see specs/rules.md)
  menuIndex: <number>,       // highlighted title-menu item, when on the title

  // The thirteen piles, each an array of cards ordered bottom to top (last = top).
  // A card is { suit, rank, color: "red" | "black", faceUp }.
  stock: [ <card>, ... ],
  waste: [ <card>, ... ],
{{#if (eq variant.slug "draw-three")}}
  wasteVisibleCount: <number>,   // how many waste cards are currently fanned face-up
{{/if}}
  foundations: [ <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile> ],
  tableau: [ <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile> ],

  // The run currently held in a drag, or null when nothing is held.
  drag: null | {
    cards: [ <card>, ... ],      // the run in hand, top card first
    source: { pile, index?, column? },
    x: <number>, y: <number>,    // the held top card's current top-left position
  },
  // The legal drop target highlighted under the held run, or null.
  dropHighlight:
    null | { pile: "foundation" | "tableau", index?: <number>, column?: <number> },

  won: <boolean>,            // true once all 52 cards are home

  // The victory cascade, present only while the game is won; null otherwise.
  cascade: null | {
    launched: <number>,      // cards launched so far
    total: <number>,         // cards to launch in all (52)
    flyers: [                // the cards currently in flight
      { suit, rank, x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number> },
    ],
    remaining: [ <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile> ],  // foundation cards not yet launched
    youWin: <boolean>,       // the YOU WIN prompt is showing
    done: <boolean>,         // every card has launched and retired
  },

  simTime: <number>,         // accumulated victory-cascade time, in seconds
}
```

A flyer's `x` and `y` are the card's top-left in the logical space, the same
values that drive its motion and its painted trail, so a caller can read where a
card is and how fast it is moving. {{#if (eq variant.slug "draw-three")}}`wasteVisibleCount` is how many of the waste's
cards are currently fanned face-up — up to the three the last stock turn brought
over (it counts down as they are played, then squares to one over the buried
cards). This field is a Draw Three affordance and is present only in this build;
the Draw One deal never fans more than a single card, so it does not report it.
{{/if}}`won` and `cascade` report the win once it is detected and the cascade as it
advances.

## The debug overlay

Provide a read-only on-screen overlay showing the game's live internal state, so
you can watch what the game is doing while you play. It is toggled with the
backtick key (`` ` ``), off by default, and never changes gameplay; it only draws.

When on, it draws over the running game, legibly, in a monospace type, at least:
the current `screen` and deal mode, the count of cards in the stock and waste and
on each foundation, the sizes of the seven tableau columns, whether a drag is in
progress and what it holds, and, during the cascade, the launched count and the
number of cards in flight, the same facts `snapshot()` reports. It is a diagnostic
layer rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep it visually plain and
clearly separate from the HUD.
